# Cursor Rule: Internationalization (i18n) for Grammy Telegram Bot

## Purpose

This project must support multiple languages from the beginning. Any new user-facing text must be internationalized. Hardcoded strings are not allowed outside the localization files.

Target stack:

- Node.js
- TypeScript
- Grammy
- @grammyjs/i18n

---

# General Rules

## Never hardcode user-facing text

❌ Bad

```ts
await ctx.reply('Welcome!');
```

```ts
await ctx.answerCallbackQuery('Saved');
```

```ts
new InlineKeyboard().text('Open profile', 'profile');
```

✅ Good

```ts
await ctx.reply(ctx.t('welcome.message'));
```

```ts
await ctx.answerCallbackQuery({
  text: ctx.t('callback.saved'),
});
```

```ts
new InlineKeyboard().text(ctx.t('buttons.profile'), 'profile');
```

---

## What must be translated

Everything visible to the user:

- reply()
- replyWithPhoto caption
- replyWithVideo caption
- replyWithDocument caption
- editMessageText()
- editMessageCaption()
- answerCallbackQuery()
- button labels
- inline keyboard labels
- reply keyboard labels
- menu commands
- command descriptions
- errors
- validation messages
- success messages
- loading texts
- onboarding
- help
- settings
- placeholders
- confirmations

Everything.

---

# Translation Keys

Keys must be descriptive.

Good

```yaml
welcome:
  title:
  description:

profile:
  title:
  stats:

buttons:
  back:
  next:
  cancel:
```

Bad

```yaml
text1:
text2:
hello:
abc:
```

---

# Folder Structure

```
src/

locales/
    en.ftl
    ru.ftl
    de.ftl

bot/
commands/
handlers/
middlewares/
utils/
```

Only localization files may contain translated text.

---

# Language Files

Use Fluent syntax.

Example

```ftl
welcome-title = Welcome!
welcome-description = Choose an action below.

buttons-profile = Profile
buttons-settings = Settings

errors-generic = Something went wrong.
```

---

# Accessing Translations

Always use

```ts
ctx.t(...)
```

Example

```ts
await ctx.reply(ctx.t('welcome-title'));
```

---

# Variables

Never concatenate strings.

❌

```ts
ctx.reply('Hello ' + user.name);
```

✅

```ftl
welcome-user = Hello, {$name}!
```

```ts
ctx.reply(
  ctx.t('welcome-user', {
    name: user.name,
  }),
);
```

---

# Pluralization

Always use Fluent plural rules.

Example

```ftl
notifications =
    { $count ->
        [one] You have {$count} notification.
       *[other] You have {$count} notifications.
    }
```

Never implement plural logic in TypeScript.

---

# Dates

Never manually format dates.

Use

- Intl.DateTimeFormat
- Intl.RelativeTimeFormat

Locale must depend on the user's language.

---

# Numbers

Use

```ts
Intl.NumberFormat;
```

Never build numbers manually.

---

# Currency

Always use

```ts
Intl.NumberFormat;
```

Example

```ts
new Intl.NumberFormat(locale, {
  style: 'currency',
  currency: 'USD',
});
```

---

# Emoji

Emoji may stay inside translations.

Example

```ftl
welcome-title = 👋 Welcome!
```

Avoid embedding emoji directly in TypeScript unless they are part of UI logic.

---

# Markdown

Formatting belongs inside translations whenever possible.

Example

```ftl
subscription-active = ✅ **Subscription is active**
```

Instead of

```ts
ctx.reply('✅ **Subscription is active**');
```

---

# HTML

Same rule.

Keep formatting in localization.

---

# Buttons

Every button label must come from i18n.

```ts
new InlineKeyboard().text(ctx.t('buttons-back'), 'back');
```

Never

```ts
.text("Back", "back")
```

---

# Errors

Every error message must be translated.

Never

```ts
throw new Error('Invalid token');
```

If the error is user-visible:

```ts
await ctx.reply(ctx.t('errors-invalid-token'));
```

Developer errors may remain in English.

---

# Logging

Logs do NOT require translation.

English is preferred.

Example

```ts
logger.info('Telegram update received');
```

---

# Internal Constants

Allowed without translation.

```ts
const CACHE_KEY = 'user_profile';
```

---

# Callback Data

Never translate callback data.

Correct

```ts
.text(ctx.t("buttons-settings"), "settings")
```

Only the visible label is translated.

---

# Commands

Commands themselves remain stable.

```
/start
/help
/settings
```

Descriptions are translated.

---

# Locale Detection

Priority

1. Saved user preference
2. Telegram language_code
3. English

---

# Default Language

English is the fallback language.

Missing translations should fall back to English.

---

# Naming Convention

Prefer kebab-case.

Examples

```
welcome-title

profile-balance

subscription-expired

buttons-back

buttons-next

errors-network
```

---

# Reusing Keys

If the same text has the same meaning, reuse the key.

Do not duplicate translations.

---

# Translation Reviews

When adding a new feature:

- add English translation
- add Russian translation
- update all supported locales
- remove unused keys
- verify no missing translations

---

# Code Review Checklist

Before finishing any task, verify:

- No hardcoded user-facing strings
- All buttons use translations
- All replies use translations
- All callback notifications use translations
- All captions use translations
- Variables use Fluent placeholders
- No string concatenation
- Locale-aware formatting is used
- New keys exist in every locale
- Translation keys are descriptive
- English remains the fallback language

If any rule is violated, refactor before completing the task.
